AB  - OBJECTIVE\nMinimally invasive techniques have been increasingly used to treat pathologic perforating veins (PVs). The goal of the current study was to summarize current literature and to determine the outcomes of treating PVs +/- great saphenous vein (GSV)/ small saphenous vein (SSV) with endovenous laser ablation (EVLA), radiofrequency ablation (RFA) and ultrasound guided sclerotherapy (USGS).\n\n\nMETHODS\nA systematic review of the literature on percutaneous treatment of PVs (33 studies) was conducted based on the "Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses" (PRISMA) guidelines and studies published up to March 2020 were included. The incidence of several endpoints at different follow up periods were calculated based on availability of data within a time frame of 3-12months from index procedure.\n\n\nRESULTS\n15 studies (N=1,677) reported on EVLA with/without sclerotherapy and/or microphlebectomy, while 12 (N=1,477) and eight (N=331) investigated the outcomes of RFA ablation with/without sclerotherapy and USGS alone, respectively. All techniques were safe in terms of periprocedural adverse events, with only a few complications occurred in each group Immediate procedural success (within 30 days) was 95% in the EVLA group, 91% in the RFS group and 58-70% in the USGS group. At 12-month follow up the occlusion rates were 89%, 77% and 83% in the EVLA, RFA and USGS groups respectively. The 12-month pooled estimated of ulcer healing between the EVLA and RFA groups were similar, although no direct comparisons were made.\n\n\nCONCLUSIONS\nTreatment of PVs with percutaneous techniques, such as EVLA, RFA and USGS is safe, and associated with high technical success rate. EVLA and RFA exhibited the most favorable outcomes. Additional research is needed to validate these results that are based on the limited level of evidence available in order to better determine the most optimal treatment approach for lower limb pathologic PVs.
ER  - 

