PY  - 2021
VL  - 10
IS  - 2
SP  - 370
EP  - 375
DO  - 10.1016/j.jvsv.2021.08.013
AN  - 34438089
AB  - INTRODUCTION\nTechnical errors are the most common preventable cause of recurrences after high ligation and stripping (HLS) procedure for treatment of great saphenous vein (GSV) incompetence. Ultrasound assisted varicose vein surgery (UAVS) utilizes intra-operative ultrasound during HLS to minimize such failures, though there are no published data regarding its use during open surgery. This study compared short-term outcomes of UAVS and endovenous laser ablation (EVLA) with a 1470 nm laser.\n\n\nMETHODS\nThis prospective randomized study was conducted from January 2019 to December 2019 wherein 40 patients who underwent UAVS under regional anesthesia were compared with an equal number of patients who underwent EVLA under tumescent anesthesia. Both groups received one week of standardized post-operative analgesia. Improvement in the pain score, venous clinical severity score (VCSS) and recurrence at 6 months and 1 year were studied.\n\n\nRESULTS\nThere was no significant difference in either clinical or radiological GSV recurrence after UAVS in comparison with EVLA at one year. Mean pain score, 8 hours post-procedure was higher in the UAVS group 3.7±1.2 vs 2.9±1.0 (p=.03) while at 1 week, the score was higher in the EVLA group 1.8±0.7 vs 1.4±0.5 (p=.01). At 6 months, VCSS improved from 9.2±3.7 to 2.4±1.4 in the UAVS group and from 9.3±3.2 to 2.1±0.8 in the EVLA group, (p=.64). At 1 year the scores were 1.3±0.7and 1.4±0.6 (p=.21) respectively.\n\n\nCONCLUSION\nUAVS has high technical success, making it a suitable alternative to EVLA using a 1470nm laser.
