TI  - Endovenous cyanoacrylate ablation for chronic venous insufficiency and varicose veins among Asians
AU  - Sally SJ Chan
AU  - Yiu Che Chan
AU  - Stewart R. Walsh
AU  - Tze Tec Chong
AU  - Edward Choke
AU  - Alok Tiwari
AU  - Tjun Yip Tang
JO  - Annals of the Academy of Medicine Singapore
PY  - 2021
VL  - 50
IS  - 3
SP  - 241
EP  - 249
DO  - 10.47102/annals-acadmedsg.2020346
AN  - 33855320
AB  - INTRODUCTION\nEndovenous cyanoacrylate glue (CAG) ablation for the treatment of chronic venous insufficiency (CVI) and varicose veins has shown non-inferior outcomes with an excellent safety profile, high patient satisfaction rate, and excellent efficacy when compared to the gold standard of endothermal ablation. A review of the current literature for CAG use in CVI showed that most studies and longer-term data are from Caucasian-based populations, which are subject to different anatomical venous variations and socio-economical contexts. This review aimed to gather the current evidence for CAG use in Asian CVI patients.\n\n\nMETHODS\nAsian studies for the use of CAG in CVI were included in this review. Successful ablation rates, quality of life improvement and novel complications such as glue hypersensitivity reactions are described, along with anatomical descriptions of superficial venous anatomy in study patients. Use of CAG in Singapore and Asia was addressed.\n\n\nRESULTS\nCAG has been gaining traction as an option for CVI treatment in Asians. In Singapore, it has been adopted with comparable low complication rates and significant improvement of quality of life after treatment. As we increase our understanding of the variations in venous anatomy in the Asian population, new techniques such as retrograde deployment of the device and use of CAG ablation for venous leg ulcers have been developed.\n\n\nCONCLUSION\nFurther robust evidence in terms of large randomised control trials along with cost effectiveness studies are needed to determine the true value of CAG ablation in the Asian setting.
