TI  - Review of Non-Thermal Non-Tumescent Endovenous Ablation using Cyanoacrylate
AU  - Srinath
AU  - G. Sharma
JO  - Surgery
PY  - 2018
VL  - 14
DO  - 10.7438/1584-9341-14-2-10
AB  - Varicose vein is a common disease entity with around 23% of people in United States [1] and 27-69% of European population [2] affected by it. Treatment of varicose veins has seen revolutionary changes in the past two decades from traditional open surgery to endovenous therapies. Open ligation and stripping of varicose veins made way for Endovenous therapies including Endovenous laser ablation (EVLA) [3,4], Radiofrequency ablation (RFA) [5] and Ultrasound guided foam sclerotherapy (UGFS) [6,7] that didn’t require general anesthesia and could be performed as an office procedure under local anesthesia. It caused less pain and discomfort, associated with improved quality of life with similar short and midterm results [8,9]. However, application of thermal ablation with EVLA or RFA required tumescent anesthesia which has its own complications and makes the procedure lengthy. Non thermal and non tumescent (NTNT) ablation techniques were thus introduced. ClariVein used to perform mechanochemical ablation (MOCA) of saphenous veins was the first device introduced which has shown good results in the initial studies [10,11]. Another recently introduced NTNT device is VenaSealTM using cyanoacrylate for ablation of refluxing veins. After the initial safety studies in animal [12,13] and then further studies in man, short term follow up data regarding the efficacy, feasibility and safety of this technique is available. The aim of this article is to review the available literature on CA ablation technique as well as critically analyze the results.
